{
  "text": "According to Newton's first law, an object will continue to move at a constant velocity unless acted upon by an external net force. This means that uniform motion, which is motion at a constant speed in a straight line, will only change if a force is applied. For example, if you're riding your bicycle at a steady pace on a straight road, you'll continue to move forward at that speed until you apply the brakes or encounter a hill, which would require you to exert a force to either stop or change your speed. This is because the force of friction and air resistance are acting against your motion, and without an additional force, these resistive forces will not change your uniform motion. [forces.txt Page 1]",
